# PART 1 — NON-NEGOTIABLE PROJECT RULES

## 1. Inspect before modifying

Before writing code, inspect the complete existing project and identify:

- Framework and application architecture.
- Existing pages, routes, layouts and reusable components.
- Current design system, styles and assets.
- Official NEXARIS logo files.
- Commodity catalogue and product data.
- Existing backend APIs and API contracts.
- Database schemas, data models and migrations.
- Authentication and authorization.
- Existing contact forms, quote forms and WhatsApp integrations.
- Email notifications and third-party integrations.
- Environment variables and deployment configuration.
- Existing tests, lint rules and build scripts.

Build an internal implementation map before making major changes.

Do not assume a technology stack, API endpoint, database schema or deployment platform that has not been confirmed by inspecting the project.

## 2. Preserve the existing application

- Retain the current framework and architecture wherever practical.
- Preserve working business logic, routes, product data, API contracts and integrations.
- Reuse existing dependencies when appropriate.
- Do not replace the application with a starter template.
- Do not overwrite real data with sample data.
- Do not remove existing features merely to simplify implementation.
- Do not invent endpoints or pretend unavailable services exist.
- Do not expose API keys, credentials, tokens or private configuration.
- Avoid destructive database changes.
- Back up significant changes or use version control where available.

If a requested feature requires new backend capabilities, implement them using the existing architecture and document the necessary migration, configuration or external dependency.

## 3. Separate verified facts from assumptions

NEXARIS operates in B2B commodity trading and sourcing, with existing website content covering categories such as spices, oilseeds and aluminium scrap.

Use the actual project data to determine what is currently offered.

Never invent:

- Certifications or registration status.
- Supplier or buyer relationships.
- Export history, shipment volumes or countries served.
- Commodity specifications, stock or prices.
- Customer testimonials or partner logos.
- Guaranteed delivery dates or quality claims.
- Legal identifiers or business credentials.

Flag information requiring business-owner verification. Never silently replace uncertain values with invented ones.

