# PART 6 — PRIVATE ENQUIRY MANAGEMENT DASHBOARD

## 12. Build a secure internal dashboard

Create a private dashboard for authorized NEXARIS personnel to manage incoming buyer and supplier enquiries.

This dashboard must not be publicly accessible.

Before implementation, inspect the existing authentication and authorization architecture.

If authentication already exists, integrate with it. If it does not, implement an appropriate secure solution consistent with the existing stack and deployment environment.

Do not create a hardcoded public admin password or expose credentials in the frontend.

### Dashboard overview

Provide useful operational summaries based on actual stored records, such as:

- New enquiries.
- Enquiries awaiting follow-up.
- RFQs under review.
- Supplier submissions awaiting review.
- Recently updated enquiries.

Show accurate counts only. Do not populate the dashboard with fake business metrics.

### Enquiry management

Authorized users should be able to:

- View enquiries.
- Search by reference, company, contact or commodity.
- Filter by status, date and enquiry type.
- Sort by relevant fields.
- Open a detailed enquiry record.
- Update status.
- Assign an internal owner if supported.
- Add private internal notes.
- Record a follow-up date.
- Review the requested commodities and specifications.
- See creation and update timestamps.
- View relevant notification status where available.

Potential statuses:

- New.
- Under Review.
- Supplier Coordination.
- Quotation Preparation.
- Quotation Sent.
- Follow-up Required.
- Won.
- Lost.
- Closed.

Use these statuses only if they fit the actual workflow. Document the permitted transitions.

### Enquiry detail page

Provide a readable record containing:

- Unique reference.
- Buyer or supplier contact information.
- Commodity requirements.
- Quantity and unit.
- Destination.
- Additional specifications.
- Current status.
- Internal notes.
- Assigned owner where supported.
- Follow-up date.
- Relevant activity history.

### Private notes and activity

Keep internal notes strictly separate from customer-visible information.

If an activity log is implemented, record meaningful actions such as status changes, assignment changes and follow-up updates.

Do not log passwords, authentication tokens or unnecessary sensitive information.

### Security requirements

- Require authentication.
- Enforce server-side authorization on every protected operation.
- Use role-based permissions if the application supports multiple roles.
- Prevent unauthorized users from reading, editing or deleting records.
- Protect internal notes and customer contact information.
- Use secure sessions or the existing authentication mechanism.
- Validate all updates on the server.
- Apply appropriate request protection and rate limiting.
- Avoid destructive deletion by default; use a safe archival approach where appropriate.
- Log administrative actions without exposing unnecessary personal data.

A hidden route is not sufficient security. Access must be enforced by the backend.

### Dashboard UI

Use a premium, restrained internal interface consistent with NEXARIS branding.

The dashboard may be more information-dense than the public website, but it must remain readable and mobile-friendly.

